<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kordofan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kordofan is a region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. It was a contested borderland over which the Darfur Sultanate and its eastern neighbours fought for control of trade and resources. Darfur's loss of Kordofan to Egyptian forces in 1821 was a significant blow that foreshadowed the sultanate's later decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kordofan.jpg" alt="Kordofan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vectorization: Carnby · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Kurdufan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nubian was spoken in central Sudan until the 19th century, Sudanese Arabic keeps many Nubian words, and rituals of Christian origin, chiefly the marking of crosses on people and objects, were still common in Omdurman, the Gezira and <b>Kordofan</b> in the 20th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The system worked well during the period of expansion, enabling Sultan Mohammed Tayrab (r. c. 1752–1785) to campaign eastward and annex <b>Kordofan</b> in the 1780s, extending the sultanate's reach towards the Nile Valley and its trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1744 Funj forces defeated an invasion by the Ethiopian emperor Iyasu II on the Dinder River, and in the late 1740s the general Muhammad Abu Likaylik conquered <b>Kordofan</b> from its Musabba’at rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Traditions say the Daju were displaced by the Tunjur around the 14th or 15th century, and Daju groups moved south and west, where Daju-speaking communities still live in Darfur, <b>Kordofan</b> and Chad.</blockquote><a href="/state/daju-kingdom">Daju kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Musabba'at were a branch of the ruling Keira family of Darfur who moved east into <b>Kordofan</b> in the 17th century after a dynastic dispute.</blockquote><a href="/state/musabbaat-sultanate">Musabba'at sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1820 to 1821 armies sent by Egypt under Muhammad Ali, led by his son Ismail Pasha, conquered the Funj Sultanate and <b>Kordofan</b>, seeking gold and slaves for the army.</blockquote><a href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a></li></ul></section>
